#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110456 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87c18ea8-af12-52bd-8420-ec24ed63de65" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Pythagorean Theorem on the Unit Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "42313322-7535-5969-ad84-958449e48c1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3480" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\dfrac{5}{9}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1 .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\dfrac{5}{9}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tx^2+y^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t        x^2+\\left(\\dfrac{5}{9}\\right)^2&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2+\\left(\\dfrac{25}{81}\\right)&=1\\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2&= 1-\\dfrac{25}{81}\\[5pt]\n\t\tx^2&= \\dfrac{56}{81}\\[5pt]\n\t\tx&= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{56}{81}}\\[5pt]\n\t        x&= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.\n}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive. So, we select the positive answer. \n\nTherefore, our final answer is $x=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110457 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "02c747c2-0eda-5c53-8e43-829f878dd278" :question/math-academy-id "e-3482" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aad0b38f5afb88c13039ac577a927e232d84905de40297ed824c966795ce7bb4.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{2}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\dfrac{2}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. \n\nSo our final answer is $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 5}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110458 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "435bc6f9-651f-511c-a972-9afe90baff32" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "bbf71023-510c-54af-9d0c-4df6744e3007" :question/math-academy-id "e-3484" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e50848432dd18a4a5dc64d4e5a5cfb8b3e14e9fed6231774dd83ead5552cd463.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\csc\\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "Again, any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{24}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{5}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{7}\\right)} = \\dfrac{7}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110459 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74d05ad6-7e4a-5c50-92d1-ca4b2f00cbb1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "ba6cc6a0-dbb8-58d2-9d02-c76310028d4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3485" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e666ddc8a8eda452abd833e963446c96de5d34dfbf76e9c92564e85a48080524.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{4}{5},$ what is $\\tan\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=\\dfrac{4}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{3}{5}.$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta},\n$$\n\nwe finally get\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)} = \\dfrac {3}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87c18ea8-af12-52bd-8420-ec24ed63de65" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Pythagorean Theorem on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a71e23-0acc-56b3-954d-83821eac7e61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288050} {:answer/id #uuid "44b4646f-0c19-57a3-bdf3-8ad3609e015a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288498} {:answer/id #uuid "7769db55-4f82-5ad4-abd8-f7f093ba576d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288856} {:answer/id #uuid "bd830b0e-ef09-5fa8-8c16-55c6bc0ac78d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289280} {:answer/id #uuid "dc81901a-30cb-5088-a113-f77b69120c2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd830b0e-ef09-5fa8-8c16-55c6bc0ac78d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289280} :answer-field/id #uuid "64e92ee8-c463-5350-91b5-0bace844e68b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "122300d6-48f4-52e5-9b3e-da4687523cb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-23216" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{1}{4}.$ What is the $y$-coordinate of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ into the above and solving for $y$ gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{16}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{15}{16} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{15}{16}} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110754 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87c18ea8-af12-52bd-8420-ec24ed63de65" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Pythagorean Theorem on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e88fcef-2ef9-533c-a8ca-bcedb24c445f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107636} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c3d912-edb9-5612-a883-39811dac0b62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107637} {:answer/id #uuid "cea0e368-fdbb-5547-b6cd-900a17d25537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{\\sqrt{31}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107638} {:answer/id #uuid "61f9b397-1a45-504c-b606-93109bc348c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107639} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9af396-f6c8-5a94-ace7-50ba124ef015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = \\frac{\\sqrt{31}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107640}] :answer-field/id #uuid "857a3db1-21fe-550d-bd38-13dc7189256d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107635}] :question/id #uuid "fe2e1b6f-5340-5b4a-a389-958e36ca570f" :question/math-academy-id "q-23274" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{7}{8}$. What is the $x$-coordinate of $A$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d900898-f644-59cb-8bd9-292f8cf96161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333108} {:answer/id #uuid "128843bf-c0d0-5b0b-9af7-5c0891c02535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333155} {:answer/id #uuid "867d9b75-2324-5dfe-830f-942376886bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334183} {:answer/id #uuid "de2d463a-06e0-5a98-b913-8a3a09131403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334926} {:answer/id #uuid "f58082e0-41ed-5179-bfb1-b391fbd71bba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "867d9b75-2324-5dfe-830f-942376886bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334183} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4eb5247-b68b-5a82-8180-4c0ffe3de0aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9c7086f-08af-5d07-8de2-8cc3da9eb077" :question/math-academy-id "q-64681" :question/problem "![](images/94/94b8650466747f6858b30adbb8962e6c91159466b570f536d4999e2b08c31b67.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{2},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/cb/cb569d4b7b76699259d2167b8c930e636e3fb794b14ef25a4d909c224235d7c3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ebb8345-8448-5af3-a7b5-f875fc57e26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{55}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333119} {:answer/id #uuid "87f8bdf1-b693-5449-95dc-41601ab98dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334192} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd4a426-2676-5c7e-b66d-c6dd8478bed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{56}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334369} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5680a5-b135-5e11-a025-14cbaf19a181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334503} {:answer/id #uuid "af99c88e-1b1e-54e6-8e94-cc6dcf9705d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{56}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac5680a5-b135-5e11-a025-14cbaf19a181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334503} :answer-field/id #uuid "2831461c-74ea-58e4-8c19-3e42bd31b998" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43fa1511-b2d2-52bf-92cb-53a58f4a78b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-65059" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d292a3598346bd05511c9c8979ba42845a88afe75a4740d8c4bdb9aa3968c87.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{5}{9},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/48/488ff32187c9a9a335e3782fb717242d899f622c43010662e28edcabe0e1bde3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{5}{9}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{9}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{5}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{25}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{56}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.$"}]}
